  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 231 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nModel guidance is aligning on a quicker passage of the backdoor cold \nfront, fully occurring by Sunday. The highest heat index values on \nSunday in the CWA will be across central and southeastern MO where \nhigher dewpoints lag behind the front, but probabilities of 100+ F \nheat index have been decreasing with each successive run in global \nensemble models. This factor decreases confidence in issuance of \nadditional heat headlines at this time, if at all. The rest of the \nCWA Sunday and CWA-wide Monday (Labor Day) will experience a break \nfrom the heat as low-level CAA and infiltrating dry air result in \nhigh temperatures in the mid-80s to low-90s F and dewpoints lowering \ninto the 60s F, even 50s F along/east of the Mississippi River on \nMonday afternoon. Although these temperatures will be cooler \ncomparatively they are still above average for early/mid-September. \nA high pressure center will also extend into the area, enforcing dry \nconditions aside from a stray shower or thunderstorm clipping \nnortheastern/central MO on Sunday afternoon/evening.\n\nTuesday through Wednesday, the upper-level ridge will shift eastward \nagain with the high pressure center quickly retreating and low-level \nsouthwesterly flow/WAA raising 850-hPa temperatures back to similar \nvalues that we are currently seeing today. This airmass has the \npotential to again yield high temperatures in the mid-90s to around \n100 F, but the longevity of these temperatures is becoming more in \nquestion as the growing consensus in model guidance is for a cold \nfront to be advancing southward into the CWA on Wednesday. \nMoisture/dewpoints will be slower to increase on Tuesday, keeping \nheat index values from rising much above air temperatures. As for \nWednesday, dewpoints should be higher with moisture pooling near the \nfront, but actual temperatures are more uncertain with the incoming \nfront and potential for associated clouds and showers/thunderstorms. \nTherefore, it is not yet clear if additional heat headlines will be \nneeded.\n\nLate Wednesday through the remainder of the next work week, a series \nof upper-level troughs will act to flatten the ridge with flow over \nthe Mid-Mississippi River Valley becoming quasi-zonal. Wednesday's \ncold front is likely to subsequently waver across the region or make \nonly gradual southward progression in this pattern. This front will \nact to separate the unseasonable heat to the south and a more \nseasonable airmass to the north with waves of showers and \nthunderstorms tracking along the front as probabilities of \nmeasurable rainfall vary between 30 and 60 percent Wednesday onward \nin ensemble model guidance. However, the many variables impacting \ntemperatures during this period inflates the NBM interquartile range \nto around 10 F, but the distribution is largely skewed to the 80s to \naround 90 F at least Thursday and Friday.",
